---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'How to Set Up WeChat Pay as a Foreigner (2026): Step-by-Step Guide';
const description = 'Step-by-step guide to setting up WeChat Pay as a foreigner. Download WeChat, bind your international credit card, verify your identity, and start paying with QR codes.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-12">
    <header class="mb-12">
      <a href="/en/payment/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline inline-flex items-center gap-1 mb-4">← Payment</a>
      <div class="text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">Payment</div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-4">
        How to Set Up WeChat Pay as a Foreigner (2026)
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        So you've got <a href="/en/payment/setup-alipay/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Alipay sorted</a>. Good. Now let's talk about WeChat Pay.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Honestly, if you only use Alipay, you'll be fine for 90%+ of your purchases. But WeChat Pay has its advantages — especially if you're planning to use WeChat for messaging (and you should, because everyone in China uses it). <strong>Having both is the gold standard.</strong>
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        One important difference: <strong>WeChat Pay is not a separate app.</strong> It's a feature inside WeChat, China's all-in-one messaging + social media + payments platform. So the first step is getting WeChat itself.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 1: Download WeChat</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Search for <strong>"WeChat"</strong> (not "Weixin" — that's the Chinese name) in your app store. The developer is "Tencent."
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li><strong>iOS:</strong> App Store</li>
        <li><strong>Android:</strong> Google Play</li>
      </ul>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 2: Create a WeChat Account</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Open the app and tap "Sign Up." You'll need:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li>Your international phone number (works the same way as Alipay)</li>
        <li>A verification code sent via SMS</li>
        <li>A profile name and photo</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Once you're in, you'll see the chat interface. Don't worry about that — WeChat Pay is tucked away in the menu.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 3: Navigate to WeChat Pay</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Tap <strong>Me</strong> (bottom right) → <strong>Services</strong> → <strong>Wallet</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        If you can't find "Wallet" in Services, you might need to update your region settings. Go to <strong>Me → Settings → General → Region</strong> and set it to "Mainland China." I know this sounds strange, but the Wallet feature is hidden if your region is set elsewhere.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 4: Add Your International Card</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        In the Wallet screen, tap <strong>Cards → Add a Card</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden mb-8 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-left">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Field</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">What to enter</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Card number</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Your Visa/Mastercard/JCB number</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Cardholder name</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">As it appears on your card</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">ID type</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Select "Passport"</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">ID number</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Your passport number</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">Expiry date</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">From your card</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">CVV</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-gray-700">The 3-digit code on the back</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Supported cards are the same as Alipay: Visa, Mastercard, JCB, Diners Club, Discover. American Express can be hit or miss.
      </p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Step 5: Set Up Your Payment PIN</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        WeChat will ask you to create a <strong>6-digit payment PIN</strong>. This is separate from your WeChat login password. Don't forget it — you'll need it for every transaction.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Identity Verification</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Before paying, you need to complete identity verification. When you first try to pay, WeChat will prompt you to upload:
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li>A photo of your passport info page</li>
        <li>A selfie or live photo for facial recognition</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        The verification usually goes through in minutes.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-100 rounded-2xl p-5 mb-8 not-prose">
        <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm font-medium mb-1">⚠️ Important: 3% fee on transactions over ¥200</p>
        <p class="text-amber-700 text-sm">WeChat Pay charges a 3% transaction fee on international card payments over ¥200 (~$28 USD). So that ¥800 dinner will cost you an extra ¥24. No way around it. For pricier purchases, some travelers prefer to use Alipay instead — it doesn't have this same fee structure for small transactions.</p>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Which Should You Use?</h2>

      <div class="space-y-3 mb-8">
        <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5 not-prose">
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">💡 Use <strong>Alipay</strong> for small daily purchases (street food, convenience stores, metro)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5 not-prose">
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">💡 Use <strong>WeChat Pay</strong> when you're already chatting on WeChat</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5 not-prose">
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">💡 Use <strong>cash</strong> as backup when your card won't work</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Extra WeChat Features Worth Knowing</h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
        Since you'll have WeChat installed anyway:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Mini Programs</strong> — Think of them as apps inside WeChat. You can order food, book taxis, buy train tickets, all without leaving WeChat.</li>
        <li><strong>QR Code scanning</strong> — Same system as Alipay. Scan to pay, or show your code to be scanned.</li>
        <li><strong>Receipts</strong> — Your payment history is in the Wallet, handy for expense tracking.</li>
      </ul>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <div class="bg-brand-50 border border-brand-100 rounded-2xl p-6 not-prose">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">More payment guides</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          <li><a href="/en/payment/payment-guide/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline">Back to the payment overview →</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en/payment/payment-fails/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline">What to do when payment fails →</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en/payment/foreign-cards/" class="text-sm text-brand-600 hover:underline">Using foreign credit cards in China →</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
